\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Cleveland and McGill (1984b) used two different types of grouped bar charts: comparisons between adjacent bars, and comparisons between separated bars. It is widely acknowledged that comparisons between separated bars (Type 3 comparisons, in Cleveland \& McGill's terminology) are more difficult and error-prone.\relax }}{31}{figure.caption.23}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig-bar-types-2}{{3.2}{31}{Cleveland and McGill (1984b) used two different types of grouped bar charts: comparisons between adjacent bars, and comparisons between separated bars. It is widely acknowledged that comparisons between separated bars (Type 3 comparisons, in Cleveland \& McGill's terminology) are more difficult and error-prone.\relax }{figure.caption.23}{}}
