\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces An illustration of the sine illusion (VanderPlas and Hofmann 2015), also known as the line-width illusion. All vertical lines are the same length, but the lines in the middle of the curve appear to be much shorter. The illusion results when implicit perceptual corrections useful for perceiving the size of objects with depth are applied to 2D stimuli with no actual depth. As 3D heuristics occasionally cause inaccurate communication when applied to 2D objects, it is reasonable to think that there might be some situations where charts making use of a realistic third dimension might be perceived more accurately than their 2D equivalents.\relax }}{33}{figure.caption.24}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig-sine-illusion}{{3.3}{33}{An illustration of the sine illusion (VanderPlas and Hofmann 2015), also known as the line-width illusion. All vertical lines are the same length, but the lines in the middle of the curve appear to be much shorter. The illusion results when implicit perceptual corrections useful for perceiving the size of objects with depth are applied to 2D stimuli with no actual depth. As 3D heuristics occasionally cause inaccurate communication when applied to 2D objects, it is reasonable to think that there might be some situations where charts making use of a realistic third dimension might be perceived more accurately than their 2D equivalents.\relax }{figure.caption.24}{}}
